STEP 2 -- "2. Start traffic into all 4 devices destined for opposite pair e.g.
           Left<->right/top<->bottom."                                      PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: tb101 has one switch and 3 host links, so traffic between 4 devices is NOT MEASURABLE here.
  The tester sent 2 routes in both directions through po1, at the same time (window E0, 12:54-12:56):
host# l2flows 100 100 /tmp/lacp-1222/flows-e0 bond0 eth2
host# linerate --tx bond0 --dst-mac 00:0d:b9:51:4d:a1 --rx eth2 --src-ip 10.37.101.34 --sport 5010 --src-macs 16 --mac-prefix 02:cc:0a:00 --rate top --secs 60
host# linerate --tx eth2 --dst-mac 00:0d:b9:51:4d:a2 --rx bond0 --sport 5011 --src-macs 16 --mac-prefix 02:cc:0b:00 --rate top --secs 60
host# capture eth3 and eth4 for 35 s, filter `ether proto 0x8809` (LACP)

  | direction           | sent      | received by host | DUT counters                        |
  | A bond0 -> eth2     | 1879545   | 1874531 (99.7%)  | po1 IN 1899557, port3.0.1 OUT 1899551 |
  | B eth2 -> bond0     | 2208726   | 2207439 (99.9%)  | port3.0.1 IN 2228726, po1 OUT 2229080 |
  Each DUT count includes the 20000 l2flows frames of that direction.
  The 6 frames not forwarded in direction A are the host's own LACPDUs, which the DUT does not forward.
  The host received fewer frames than the DUT sent because of the host's own receive buffer:
  eth2 rx_fifo_errors 5054; eth3 + eth4 rx_fifo_errors 1291 = bond0 RX dropped 1291. The DUT lost nothing.
  Direction A arrived 50.5% on port1.0.1 and 49.5% on port2.0.1, so both LAG links carried traffic.
  l2flows: all 4 flows delivered, 0 duplicates.
host# lacpdu.py on the eth3 and eth4 captures
